index fef4eda..45e63d8 100644
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -38,6 +38,18 @@ ordering for all assertions:
 Check the source code of the assertions to find out details about the additional
 available placeholders.
 
+Because the message is applied as a `sprintf()` format string, a literal percent sign
+has to be written `%%`:
+
+```
+Assert::true($ok, 'Coverage must be 100%%.');
+```
+
+A message whose placeholders cannot be applied - an unknown specifier, or more
+placeholders than the assertion supplies values for - is reported unformatted rather
+than raising out of the assertion, so every failure still arrives as an
+`InvalidArgumentException`.
+
 Installation
 ------------
 
@@ -139,6 +151,14 @@ Method                                                | Description
 `oneOf($value, array $values, $message = '')`         | Check that a value is one of a list of values (alias of `inArray`)
 `notOneOf($value, array $values, $message = '')`      | Check that a value is not one of a list of values (alias of `notInArray`)
 
+The five ordering assertions - `greaterThan`, `greaterThanEq`, `lessThan`, `lessThanEq` and
+`range` - only compare operands that can be ordered against one another: two numbers, two
+strings, two booleans, two arrays or two objects. A pair PHP would decide by coercing one side
+(an object against a number, an array against a number, a non-numeric string against a number)
+is reported as a failed assertion rather than compared, and `NAN` is ordered against nothing at
+all. `eq`, `notEq`, `same` and `notSame` are unaffected: they are documented as `==` and `===`
+and keep exactly those semantics.
+
 ### String Assertions
 
 You should check that a value is a string with `Assert::string()` before making
@@ -172,6 +192,13 @@ Method                                              | Description
 `email($value, $message = '')`                      | Check that a string is a valid e-mail address
 `notWhitespaceOnly($value, $message = '')`          | Check that a string contains at least one non-whitespace character
 
+`regex` and `notRegex` compile the pattern you give them, and a pattern that does not compile is
+reported as such - `The pattern "/[/" is not a valid regular expression: ...` - rather than as a
+verdict about the value. A valid pattern that cannot be matched at all, because the subject
+exhausts a PCRE limit or is not valid UTF-8 under `/u`, is reported as
+`The pattern "/./u" could not be matched against the value: ...` carrying PCRE's own reason. A
+custom message describes the value, so it is not used for either failure.
+
 ### File Assertions
 
